<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="5d588b4d" lines="10-28">
<description>
Expand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.

Input structure:
- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.
- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.
- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.

Rule:
- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths
  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.
- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.
- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row
  with background pixels.

Key insight:
The output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length
diamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="342dd610" lines="18-36">
<description>
Move every foreground pixel by the fixed offset indicated by its color.

Input structure:
- The grid has a unique dominant background color.
- The foreground is a sparse set of colored pixels.
- Each foreground color is one of the trained motion colors: orange, red,
  maroon, or blue.

Rule:
- Clear the canvas to the background color.
- Translate orange pixels up 2 cells, red pixels left 2 cells, maroon
  pixels down 2 cells, and blue pixels right 1 cell.
- Preserve each moved pixel's original color.

Key insight:
The foreground color is the motion instruction; position only determines
where that color-specific displacement lands.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="fbf15a0b" lines="49-69">
<description>
Crop out the grid half containing the two aligned marker pixels, then erase the markers to background.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color and one non-background marker color
  that appears as exactly two cells.
- The marker cells are aligned either in one row or one column and both lie
  in the same half along that direction.
- Other non-background pixels are content inside one or both halves.

Rule
- If the markers share a row, keep the left or right half whose columns
  contain them.
- If the markers share a column, keep the top or bottom half whose rows
  contain them.
- In the kept half, recolor the marker pixels to the background color.

Key insight
- The two marker pixels are not part of the picture; their alignment and
  half-position identify which half of the doubled canvas is the answer.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="7d7772cc" lines="70-92">
<description>
Move each source pixel toward the separator when it matches its aligned reference pixel, otherwise to the far edge.

Input structure:
- A single full separator line is part of a rectangular frame; the frame's
  extra cells mark the reference side.
- On the reference side, one row or column of colored pixels is aligned
  with one row or column of source pixels on the opposite side.
- Each side has its own dominant background color.

Rule:
- Compare each source pixel with the reference pixel on the same row or column.
- If their colors match, place the source pixel in the cell adjacent to the
  separator on the source side.
- If their colors differ, place the source pixel on the far outer edge along
  the same row or column.
- Erase the original source pixels to the source-side background; keep the
  frame and reference pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
The extra frame cells identify which side is the reference; equality with the
aligned reference pixel decides whether each source pixel moves inward or outward.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="62593bfd" lines="32-46">
<description>
Sort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.

Rule:
- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).
- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).
- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).
- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.

Key insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="21897d95" lines="283-301">
<description>
Repaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.

Input structure:
- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.
- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.
- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.
- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.

Rule:
- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.
- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.
- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.
- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.
- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.

Key insight:
- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="5545f144" lines="191-207">
<description>
Move a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.

Input structure:
- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.
- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.

Rule:
- Start from the rightmost frame.
- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable
  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the
  nearest one.
- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.
- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.

Key insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="34cfa167" lines="94-117">
<description>
Complete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite
  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.
- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its
  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.

Rule:
- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied
  larger rectangle.
- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right
  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and
  bottom borders in the first right-control color.
- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom
  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders
  in the first below-control color.

Key insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic
frame patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the
missing two anchor copies.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="c61be7dc" lines="42-57">
<description>
Flatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.

Input structure:
- The dominant color is the background.
- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.
- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.

Rule:
- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.
- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.
- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.

Key insight:
- The guide bars supply only the orientation and center; the interior object's pixel count supplies the length of the final one-cell-thick line.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="2faf500b" lines="30-44">
<description>
Remove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.

Input structure
- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.
- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.

Rule
- Erase all marker pixels.
- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.

